      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "1945859837042041342",
      "url": "https://x.com/pdokprodo/status/1945859837042041342",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/pdokprodo/status/1945859837042041342",
      "text": "MMDA CHIEF’S BLAME GAME MISSES THE POINT\n\nMMDA Acting Chair Romando Artes’ recent statement blaming the Dolomite Beach for flooding along Taft Avenue is not only technically questionable—it reeks of political deflection.\n\nLet’s set the record straight.\n\n1. The Dolomite Beach is part of a Supreme Court-mandated cleanup.\n\nThe Manila Bay Rehabilitation Program stems from a 2008 Mandamus by the Supreme Court ordering 13 agencies—including the MMDA—to clean up and rehabilitate Manila Bay until its waters are safe for swimming. The Dolomite Beach, while controversial to some, was a visible and symbolic part of this larger compliance effort—one that millions of Metro residents and tourists have come to enjoy.\n\n2. Why blame success?\n\nInstead of seeing the dolomite development as a burden, why not see it for what it is—one of the few successful and visible outcomes of a long-overdue government effort? It attracted local and international attention, raised public awareness, and boosted the morale of environmental campaigns.\n\n3. If the sewerage system is failing, fix it.\n\nIf indeed the diversion of water flow toward treatment canals is causing flooding, then that’s a failure of engineering planning, not of beachfront beautification. Why hasn’t MMDA improved the capacity of the canal system instead of scapegoating a feature project? Artes, as MMDA chair, had ample time and authority to resolve drainage limitations. Blaming a beach for blocked outflows is like blaming curtains for a leaky roof.\n\n4. Stop politicizing a Supreme Court order.\n\nLet’s not forget—it was the Duterte administration that had the political will to operationalize the Court’s ruling. The same administration boldly shut down Boracay to fix its environmental mess, despite backlash. Say what you will about its shortcomings, but it did act where previous governments hesitated.\n\nSo, the real question is: Is MMDA’s sudden critique of the dolomite beach technical, or political?\n\nBecause from where we stand, blaming Duterte-era projects for current problems is becoming a lazy narrative—a convenient distraction from present-day inaction.\n\nIf the current administration is truly committed to environmental rehabilitation, it should build on what has been started—not demolish it with talking points.\n_Butch Ruiz Baliao",
